<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bačka</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bácska</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bačka is a region of the Pannonian Plain between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tisza">Tisza</a>, divided between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>'s province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vojvodina">Vojvodina</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. A fertile lowland, it was part of medieval Hungary and came under Ottoman rule in the 16th century until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-karlowitz">Treaty of Karlowitz</a> in 1699, after which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> rule brought settlers including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, Germans, Hungarians and Slovaks. Most of it passed to the Kingdom of Serbs, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a> in 1918.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/backa.jpg" alt="Bačka" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pasztilla aka Attila Terbócs · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Baja,_v%C3%A1rosh%C3%A1za_2021_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
